from __future__ import annotations

NAME = "How We Know the Stars"
AMBIENCE = "Singing Bowl"

# A soothing sleep-lecture about how humanity measured the distances to the stars, by parallax, light and patience.
SECTIONS = [['You do not need to do anything now.', 'There is nowhere you have to be and nothing you have to solve.', 'Let your body settle into whatever holds you.', 'Feel the weight of yourself sinking, softly, into the bed.', 'Let your shoulders drop a little lower than they were.', 'There is no rush here, and there never was.', 'Breathe in slowly, gently, and let the breath leave on its own.', 'Let the next breath be even slower than the last.', 'Your eyes can close now, if they have not already closed.', 'Imagine you are lying somewhere open and quiet, far from any light.', 'Above you, the dark is wide and soft and patient.', 'You are simply going to lie here and look up, and let your mind grow still.'], ['For most of human history, no one knew how far away the stars were.', 'People looked up, as you are looking up now, and simply wondered.', 'The stars seemed like tiny lights fixed to a great dark dome.', 'All anyone knew, deep down, was that they were very far.', 'Far enough that no one could ever hope to walk there.', 'The riddle was this, and you can hold it lightly as you drift.', 'How do you measure something you can never reach or touch?', 'You cannot stretch a string to a star and read off its length.', 'Yet there was one quiet trick, hidden in the way we see.', 'Hold up one finger at arms length, and close one eye, then the other.', 'Watch how your finger seems to jump against the far wall.', 'Things that are near seem to shift more than things that are far.'], ['That small jump is called parallax, and it is a doorway.', 'From the size of that gentle shift, you can find the distance.', 'The Earth, you see, does not stand still beneath you as you sleep.', 'It drifts in a great slow circle around the Sun, all year long.', 'In summer you are on one side of that circle, in winter the other.', 'So across the year, you yourself become two eyes, far apart.', 'You look at the stars from one side of your orbit, then the other.', 'And the very nearest stars seem to shift, just the tiniest amount.', 'Not a leap, not a jump, but a whisper of a shift against the rest.', 'The far stars hold still, like that distant wall, and the near ones drift.', 'It is the gentlest motion in all the sky, almost too small to see.', 'Smaller than the width of a hair held far across a quiet room.'], ['For a long time the shift was simply too tiny for anyone to catch.', 'People searched for it patiently, year after year, and could not find it.', 'But the not-finding was its own quiet answer, soft and humbling.', 'If even the nearest stars barely shifted, the stars must be terribly far.', 'So they kept watching, and kept measuring, with more and more care.', 'In the year 1838, a patient man named Friedrich Bessel finally saw it.', 'He watched a faint star called 61 Cygni, night after night after night.', 'He measured its position with enormous, loving care.', 'Slowly, across the year, he saw it shift, just barely, and shift back.', 'From that whisper of a shift, he found how far the star truly was.', 'It was the first time a human ever measured the distance to a star.', 'After all those thousands of years of wondering, one quiet answer arrived.'], ['The distance was so vast that ordinary words could not hold it.', 'No miles, no numbers we use for roads, could carry such a length.', 'So we measure the stars in light-years, and it is a tender idea.', 'A light-year is how far light travels in one whole year.', 'Light is the fastest thing there is, and never seems to rest.', 'Yet to cross from us to even the nearest star takes it years.', 'Bessels little star lay many light-years off across the dark.', 'And that means the light you see from it left long, long ago.', 'To look up at a star is to look softly back in time.', 'You are seeing not the star as it is, but the star as it was.', 'The light reaching your eyes set out before this night was ever born.', 'You can rest in that ancient light, the way the old watchers did.'], ['The shift grows too small for stars that lie farther away.', 'So we needed new rungs, a little higher up, to reach the rest.', 'Some stars do not shine evenly, but brighten and dim, over and over.', 'They swell and fade in a slow, faithful rhythm, like breathing.', 'A woman named Henrietta Leavitt studied them, patiently, by the thousands.', 'She found that timing a stars heartbeat reveals its true brightness.', 'Farther still, an old star sometimes ends in a great, gentle blaze.', 'Such blazes shine with a known and steady flame, scattered across the dark.', 'Each method reaches a little further than the one before it.', 'We call this gentle stack the cosmic distance ladder.', 'Each rung rests on the one below, with trust and care.', 'We measured the unreachable with patience, geometry, and light.'], ['There is a deep humility in how all of this was done.', 'We never touched a single star, and we never will.', 'We only watched, and waited, and measured with great care.', 'The universe did not shout its distances to us.', 'It whispered them, slowly, to those who watched with love.', 'You can let the wondering grow quiet now.', 'Let the names and the numbers drift away into the dark.', 'You do not have to know anything at all to rest beneath the stars.', 'The old light still falls on you, soft and ancient and kind.', 'Let each breath leave a little longer than the one before.', 'There is nothing left to measure, and nowhere left to go.', 'Drift now, softly, under the patient and ancient stars, and sleep.']]
